  "findings": [
    "Un-merging every block recovers the whole inflation: 0.010452 merged against 0.009362, which IS the checkpoint's shipped error. The gap is 0.001090, 1.1164x.",
    "THERE IS NO HOT SUBSET, only a mildly concave curve. Block inflation spans 1.131x across all 50 -- no hotspot -- so worst-first buys a modest premium over an arbitrary choice rather than a big one: worst-5 recovers 15.3% against 10% for any 5, and the premium peaks at 18.2 points around the halfway mark (worst-25 recovers 68.2%). So targeting is a COMPUTE optimisation, not a fidelity one: the full recovery is available at +2.4% FLOPs on the linears, and any subset trades fidelity away to save a fraction of that. Whoever picks a subset should say which of the two they are buying.",
    "The 7-block sample's `worst-first 49, 7, 24, 16` is half right and reads as advice to target. Blocks 49 and 7 do rank 1st and 5th of 50; blocks 24 and 16 are mid-pack at 17th and 16th. Its `least worth it: 32 and 40` holds -- they rank 46th and 44th, in a quiet band running roughly 31-36.",
    "INFLATION AND STORED ERROR RANK KINDS OPPOSITELY. attn.qkv_proj has the highest inflation (1.1636) and the lowest error before PDD (0.00883); attn.out_proj has the highest error before PDD (0.01041) and nearly the lowest inflation (1.0990). 'Which kind quantises worst' and 'which kind PDD damages most' are different questions and the answers are reversed."
  ],
